{"evt":"note_created","id":"note-mr3v9la2scg","workspace":"/Users/imyu/Desktop/zonoid","title":"duck harness vision and anti text dump tricks","summary":"Concrete techniques from Tufalabs/duck-harness (ARC-AGI-3 milestone 1 winner) for vision + avoiding board text dumps. VISION: render grid 1px/cell with fixed 16-color RGB palette, then NEAREST-resize ×16 (default MULTIMODAL_UPSCALE=16, so each cell = exactly one 16x16 Qwen vision patch; 64x64 grid → 1024x1024 PNG data URL) injected in every user turn as \"Current grid image:\". ANTI-DUMP: (1) raw numeric grid intentionally NOT exposed — frame exposes only .ascii/.segmentation/.step/.level/.shape; (2) ascii = 1 letter per cell with a color legend, and prompt restricts it to reading small local regions only; (3) primary board view is connected-component segmentation: per-object id, color char, translation-invariant shape hash (sha1 of color+cells normalized to bbox origin — tracks objects across frames), pixel count, boundary as clockwise CORNER points only (not cell lists), containment children tree, plus adjacency list; (4) hard tool-output cap ~1024 tokens (≈4KB chars) with explicit cut-off notice; (5) prompt bans printing full boards — \"return only compact derived summaries: object lists, diffs, coordinates, counts, tiny local crops\"; (6) carried world model is 7 structured slots (world/goal/action model, recent findings, open questions, plan, cross-level notes), not one free-text tag. Code refs: inference/agent/vision_context.py, inference/utils/segmentation.py, inference/agent/prompts.py in the duck-harness repo.","category":null,"tags":["arc-agi-3","vision","context-engineering","harness"],"created_by":"main","valid_from":"2026-07-02T18:57:27.290Z","vec":[-0.006738028023391962,-0.0054440489038825035,-0.031446024775505066,-0.021555766463279724,0.14022447168827057,-0.056823987513780594,0.006229744758456945,0.023417290300130844,0.048646945506334305,-0.033702827990055084,-0.019060727208852768,-0.0585671253502368